<TopicPage slug="subjects/respiratory-therapy/foundational/cardiopulmonary-assessment">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Respiratory therapy begins with assessment: reading the cardiopulmonary system from vitals,
    blood gases, imaging and physical findings. The logic is the same as any instrument panel —
    the body's gas-exchange machinery reports its status continuously, in numbers and signs, and
    the therapist's foundational skill is reading that panel correctly and knowing the physiology
    behind every needle. A patient who is breathing 28 times a minute with a low blood pressure
    and a silent left chest is not "sick in general"; they are telling you something specific
    about their lungs and circulation, and the assessment toolkit is how you hear them.
  </p>
  <p>
    The blood gas is the discipline's signature test: a few numbers that describe how well the
    lungs are exchanging oxygen and clearing CO₂, and how the body's chemistry is coping. But
    the gas is only readable on top of anatomy and physiology — you must know what the alveolus
    does, what haemoglobin carries, and how the kidneys answer the lungs before any of the
    numbers mean anything. That is why the foundational unit pairs them: cardiopulmonary anatomy
    and physiology first, then the interpretation skills built over it, then imaging and labs
    as the wider evidence base.
  </p>
  <p>
    Everything here also frames the profession's identity: assessment is the therapy's aiming
    system. Every later unit — oxygen devices, ventilation modes, weaning — adjusts therapy
    against this assessment, and a ventilator change without a blood-gas rationale is a guess.
    The profession's value is exactly that it doesn't guess.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="reading an arterial blood gas, systematically" widget="equationStep" config={abgLearnCfg} />

  <h2>Cardiopulmonary anatomy and physiology</h2>
  <p>
    Follow a breath. Air enters through conducting airways (trachea, bronchi, bronchioles) that
    warm, humidify and filter it but exchange no gas — about 150 mL of this <em>anatomic dead
    space</em> must be re-filled with every breath before fresh air reaches the alveoli. At the
    alveoli, some 300 million thin-walled sacs wrapped in capillaries, the membrane separating
    air from blood is under a micrometre thick: oxygen diffuses down its gradient into blood,
    CO₂ diffuses out, and the whole exchange completes in about a quarter of a second. The
    muscle of breathing is the diaphragm; contracting it drops thoracic pressure, air flows in,
    and relaxation plus elastic recoil pushes it out — which is why a patient whose lungs have
    lost elasticity (emphysema) must <em>work</em> to exhale.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two circulations matter and they answer different questions. <strong>Ventilation</strong> is
    air reaching alveoli; <strong>perfusion</strong> is blood reaching them. Their match — the
    V/Q ratio — is the heart of respiratory pathophysiology: blood flowing past unventilated
    alveoli (pneumonia, atelectasis) is a <em>shunt</em>, oxygen-resistant; air reaching
    unperfused alveoli (pulmonary embolism) is dead-space ventilation. The body's elegant
    partial fix is hypoxic vasoconstriction, steering blood away from poorly ventilated regions.
  </p>
  <p>
    Transport completes the physiology. Oxygen travels mostly bound to haemoglobin — each gram
    carries about 1.34 mL of O₂, so arterial blood holds near 20 mL per 100 mL, versus under
    1 mL dissolved — and the binding is drawn as the S-shaped oxyhaemoglobin dissociation curve:
    a flat plateau (saturation barely falls until PaO₂ drops below about 60 mmHg) protecting
    loading, and a steep descent in tissue unloading O₂ where it is needed. Acid, heat and CO₂
    all shift the curve right (easier unloading), which links the two gases' transport. CO₂
    travels mostly as bicarbonate: it enters red cells, carbonic anhydrase converts it to
    carbonic acid, which dissociates — and this chemistry is why CO₂ is both the metabolic
    currency and the acid–base lever the blood gas reads.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> breathing hard fixes oxygen problems. Ventilation moves CO₂
    fast, but oxygen uptake is perfusion-limited and haemoglobin-limited — a patient hyperventilating
    on 100 mL of blood bypassing their alveoli (a shunt) will not raise their PaO₂ much at all.
    That asymmetry is also why the ABG can separately implicate the lungs (CO₂, ventilation) and
    the metabolism (HCO₃⁻) — a diagnostic elegance the therapist works with every shift.
  </p>

  <h2>Blood gas interpretation</h2>
  <p>
    The arterial blood gas reports four principal values — pH (normal 7.35–7.45), PaCO₂
    (35–45 mmHg), HCO₃⁻ (22–26 mEq/L) and PaO₂ — and the interpretation method is a fixed
    five-step habit, drilled until automatic. First, pH names the crisis (acidaemia or
    alkalaemia). Second, PaCO₂ is the respiratory term: above 45 means underventilation holding
    an acid load; below 35 means the lungs are blowing off acid. Third, HCO₃⁻ is the metabolic
    term: below 22 is metabolic acid, above 26 a metabolic alkalosis. Fourth, match the
    offending value's direction to the pH — the matching abnormality is the <em>primary</em>
    disorder, the other term is <em>compensating</em>. Fifth, check whether the compensation is
    the right size; if not, a second disorder is hiding.
  </p>
  <p>
    Work one. pH 7.34, PaCO₂ 65 mmHg, HCO₃⁻ 35 mEq/L: pH is low (acidaemia), CO₂ is high in
    the same direction, so the primary disorder is <strong>respiratory acidosis</strong> —
    underventilation (think COPD exacerbation, opiate sedation, neuromuscular weakness). But
    the bicarbonate is also high, and the kidneys only raise HCO₃⁻ meaningfully over days: this
    is <em>chronic</em> respiratory acidosis with renal compensation, the signature of a
    long-standing CO₂ retainer, and it changes therapy — this patient's drive to breathe may
    partly depend on their hypoxia, so correcting their oxygen too enthusiastically is
    dangerous. Now try pH 7.22, PaCO₂ 60, HCO₃⁻ 24: same high CO₂, but a normal bicarbonate —
    an <em>acute</em> respiratory acidosis, hours old, and an emergency that needs ventilation
    now rather than adaptation. Drag the sliders into each of the four classic pictures and
    watch the classification logic snap into place.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="blood gas values → acid–base classification" widget="sliderLab" config={abgCfg} />

  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> compensation "fixes" the disorder. Compensating systems push
    pH back toward normal but never fully normalise it, and each system has limits and
    timescales — lungs compensate metabolic disorders within minutes, kidneys compensate
    respiratory disorders over days. A fully normal pH with wild gas values is the signature of
    a mixed disorder, not health.
  </p>

  <h2>The physical examination: four moves, many answers</h2>
  <p>
    Before any number, the therapist assesses by hand and ear, and the four classical moves
    each have a physiological reason. <strong>Inspection</strong>: breathing rate, depth and
    pattern (a patient using neck muscles and flaring nostrils is in respiratory distress);
    chest shape (a barrel chest suggests hyperinflation); cyanosis and clubbing of the
    fingers (chronic hypoxaemia's signatures). <strong>Palpation</strong>: chest expansion
    compared side to side (asymmetry points at collapse, effusion or pneumonia on the quiet
    side), tactile fremitus (vibration of spoken words felt through the chest — increased
    over consolidated lung, decreased over fluid). <strong>Percussion</strong>: tapping the
    chest and listening to the ring — hollow over normal air-filled lung, dull over
    consolidation or fluid, drumlike (tympanic) over a pneumothorax. <strong>Auscultation</strong>:
    breath sounds through the stethoscope — vesicular (normal), bronchial (harsh, over
    consolidation), absent (over fluid or air), and the added sounds: crackles (fluid in
    airways — oedema, pneumonia, fibrosis), wheezes (narrowed airways — asthma, COPD),
    and the pleural friction rub (inflamed pleura grating). The examination narrows the
    hypothesis before the imaging and the gas confirm it: dull percussion plus bronchial
    breath sounds plus crackles is pneumonia's signature long before the chest film.
  </p>

  <h2>Chest imaging and labs</h2>
  <p>
    The chest X-ray is the workhorse image, read systematically: airway, bones, cardiac
    silhouette, diaphragms, lung fields, and everything else (tubes, lines, devices). Its
    physiological logic is density — air is black, tissue is grey, metal is white — so
    <em>infiltrates</em> (pneumonia, oedema, blood) whiten lung fields; a <em>pneumothorax</em>
    shows as a black, vessel-free zone with a pleural edge; hyperinflated, flattened diaphragms
    betray obstructive disease; an enlarged heart silhouette starts the cardiac conversation.
    CT adds cross-sectional detail — pulmonary emboli (CT pulmonary angiography), interstitial
    disease patterns, tumour staging — and ultrasound is increasingly bedside-first for
    pleural effusion and pneumothorax.
  </p>
  <p>
    Labs complete the picture and cross-check the gas. Haemoglobin determines oxygen content,
    so anaemia can dyspnoeic a patient with normal saturations; electrolytes travel with
    acid–base disorders (chloride gaps, potassium shifts); BNP points at cardiac causes of
    breathlessness; troponin flags myocardial injury; D-dimer helps exclude embolism in
    low-risk patients; sputum cultures and inflammatory markers (WBC, CRP, procalcitonin)
    shape infection stories. The assessment habit that ties it together: no single value
    diagnoses — vitals, examination, gas, imaging and labs are one triangulated argument about
    what the cardiopulmonary system is doing.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The alveolar gas equation quantifies the gas exam's hardest question</strong> — is
    the hypoxaemia from hypoventilation alone or from lung pathology? Alveolar PO₂ (PAO₂) is
    computed from inspired oxygen and CO₂: on room air, PAO₂ ≈ 150 − (PaCO₂ / 0.8) mmHg. The
    <strong>A–a gradient</strong> — the gap between that calculated alveolar value and the
    measured arterial PaO₂ — is small when the only problem is underventilation (pure
    hypoventilation keeps the gradient normal) and large when V/Q mismatch, shunt or diffusion
    impairment is present. That single subtraction often decides between "the patient just
    stopped breathing adequately" and "the lungs themselves are failing".
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Non-invasive monitoring extends the gas.</strong> Pulse oximetry reads saturation
    from light absorption but has sharp edges: it says nothing about CO₂ or pH, reads falsely
    high in carbon monoxide poisoning, and loses accuracy with poor perfusion and dark skin
    pigmentation. Capnography — measuring exhaled CO₂ — has become the ventilator's vital sign:
    end-tidal CO₂ tracks alveolar ventilation beat by breath, and its sudden collapse is the
    classic signal of embolism or circuit disaster. Arterial sampling remains the ground truth;
    the modern skill is knowing which instrument answers which question, and when the cheap
    monitor is lying.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Mixed acid–base states are where mastery shows.</strong> A septic patient can be
    simultaneously lactic (metabolic acidosis) and hyperventilating (respiratory alkalosis from
    both compensation and sepsis-driven drive); a COPD patient on diuretics can carry chronic
    respiratory acidosis <em>plus</em> metabolic alkalosis. Winter's formulas and the anion gap
    (normal 8–12 mEq/L) put numbers on what "expected compensation" means, and the therapist
    who runs those checks finds the second disorder that a glance at pH would miss. Every
    later unit — oxygen devices, ventilation modes, weaning trials — adjusts therapy against
    this assessment, which is why this page is the foundation the rest of the programme stands on.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Bedside function tests complete the assessment set.</strong> Pulse oximetry and
    the gas describe a moment; functional tests describe capacity. The classic is the
    six-minute walk: how far a patient can ambulate in six minutes at their own pace,
    with saturations and dyspnoea scored throughout — a cheap, standardised probe of
    exercise tolerance that predicts outcomes in COPD and pulmonary hypertension.
    Respiratory muscle strength (maximal inspiratory pressure) answers the neuromuscular
    question — can the diaphragm still generate adequate force? — which matters for
    weaning decisions. And cough assessment (peak cough flow) answers a question the gas
    never will: can the patient clear their own secretions, the determinant of airway
    safety in neuromuscular disease? Each test is simple; each converts a subjective
    complaint ("I get breathless") into a tracked number — and tracking numbers against
    interventions is the discipline's whole method, from first assessment to discharge.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> NBRC TMC content outline; Cairo, <em>Pilbeam's Mechanical Ventilation</em>; Des Jardins, <em>Cardiopulmonary Anatomy &amp; Physiology</em>.</p>
